NAME
    web-analyze - passive Chromium/CDP website evidence and Markdown analysis

SYNOPSIS
    web-analyze <http(s)-url> <analysis-path> [--scroll]

DESCRIPTION
    Launches a fresh headless Chromium session through the existing native-CDP
    web-analyze.lib.js recorder. The command waits for a rendered document,
    waits for a heuristic document-settled condition before the initial
    full rendered-DOM snapshot, then acquires a read-only deep-rest phase
    (CDP DOM tree including Shadow DOM, layout/style snapshots, native MHTML,
    CSSOM/style source, runtime element/storage details, listener locations,
    frame targets, redacted network event metadata and archived responses).
    It inventories controls/forms/headings and best-effort React runtime
    markers without issuing user input. With the optional --scroll operand,
    it then scrolls the document and nested scroll containers, recording
    additional bounded states after settlement checks.
    It then stops Chromium,
    allowing the recorder's normal network/resource drain, and generates
    analytical Markdown documents from the acquired evidence.

    This is observation, not autonomous proof of every application behavior.
    The command does not click buttons, submit forms, authenticate, follow
    links, modify account state, bypass challenges or claim unseen states.
    Even an optional scroll sweep cannot exhaust a dynamic React app.

OPERANDS
    http(s)-url       Required absolute HTTP or HTTPS URL to navigate.
    analysis-path     Parent folder for analysis runs. Created if missing.
                      Each invocation creates a unique private run-* folder;
                      pre-existing analyses are preserved unchanged.
    --scroll          Optional: perform passive automated scrolling after
                      completing the initial deep resting-page capture.
                      Default: no programmatic scroll or user input.

OUTPUT
    Prints the absolute path to the generated README.md on stdout.
    Progress and diagnostics go to stderr.

    Inside each run-* folder:
      README.md        Analysis index, evidence counts, confidentiality notice
      DOM.md           Observed routes, headings, forms and interactive controls
      NETWORK.md       Request catalog (bounded summary), resource outcomes
      SCRIPTS.md       Compiled JS assets and best-effort React/Next evidence
      FLOWS.md         Mermaid diagram and table of observed scroll transitions
      LIMITATIONS.md   Coverage limits, missing resource outcomes and warnings
      rest/target-graph-initial.json
                       Discovered/attached target and execution contexts
      rest/target-graph-final.json
                       Refreshed target hierarchy and unresolved attachments
      rest/            Rest-phase indexed evidence, cookies, per-target
                       local/session/IndexedDB/Cache Storage, DOM/layout/CSS,
                       listeners, runtime environment and native MHTML
      states/          Per-state rendered HTML/text/controls in JSON, selected PNGs
                       dom-rest-initial.html / dom-rest-final.html (full outerHTML)
                       dom-rest-{initial,final}.json (settlement evidence)
      cdp-capabilities.json  Browser version, schema discovery status,
                             supported domains/commands/events
      cdp-protocol.json      Version-specific native Chromium CDP schema;
                             only present when discovery succeeds
      cdp-wire.jsonl         Every CDP packet observed by the connected
                             debugger, including unhandled events and results
      network-details.jsonl  Private, full CDP HTTP/request and live-message details
      resources.jsonl  Complete CDP response capture index
      resources/       Decoded HTTP response body artifacts by content hash
      scripts.jsonl    Complete Debugger.scriptParsed source index
      scripts/         JavaScript source artifacts by content hash
      *.jsonl          Lifecycle, DOM, interaction, network and runtime events
      summary.json     Recording totals and incomplete-resource counters
      profile/         Dedicated Chromium browsing profile for this run

    DOM quiet observation also watches discovered open Shadow DOM trees and
    readable same-origin iframe documents, but inaccessible frames cannot
    be treated as completely observed.

    A DOM snapshot is considered settled when document.readyState is
    complete, document fonts are loaded, DOM mutations and network events
    have been quiet for 1,500 ms, and no HTTP requests or body reads remain
    in flight. If requests remain open after 4,500 ms without a network
    event and DOM mutations are quiet, the snapshot is saved as
    dom-quiet-network-pending rather than settled. This avoids indefinite
    waits on persistent streams while reporting unresolved network work.
    Initial settlement is allowed up to 15 seconds; subsequent states
    up to 6 seconds, within an overall 45-second exploration budget.
    These are heuristics, not proof of final application state.
    If settlement times out (e.g. continuous DOM updates), the command
    still saves best-effort DOM evidence with status timeout/unavailable.
    The rest phase captures observed browser cookies (including HttpOnly)
    and readable localStorage/sessionStorage values. It freezes a copy of the
    private CDP network detail stream at this point. Values may include active
    credentials; do not transmit the output or publish it to GitHub.
    Deep resting-page evidence is captured before any programmatic scroll.
    The default command stops at this boundary; --scroll enables an additional
    exploration phase with a separate time budget.
    The existing bounded scroll sweep still follows as a separate operation.
    Rest artifacts include per-artifact saved/unavailable outcomes and SHA-256.
    A REST status of partial must be inspected rather than interpreted as
    proof of complete source acquisition. Full initial/final rendered HTML
    is stored separately from bounded exploratory JSON snapshots.

    The bounded exploration captures at most 29 states, with up to 1,200
    controls and 2 MB rendered HTML per-state JSON. Its final Markdown tables
    summarize a bounded prefix; the raw indexes retain all indexed outcomes.
    Recursively attached iframe/worker targets are inventoried with their
    parent target, execution contexts, enabled instrumentation domains and
    errors. Worker globals have separate snapshots instead of page DOM/CSS.
    Chromium can render cross-origin frames in one process or a separate
    OOPIF, so frame tree and target graph remain distinct evidence.

    The raw packet ledger does not filter by known CDP event names; it
    includes outgoing commands and incoming responses/notifications while
    the debugger is connected. The summary reports packets and local write
    errors. A complete ledger of *observed* packets is not a guarantee that
    disabled CDP domains, unattached targets or future activity were observed.
    The protocol schema is retrieved from this browser's local debugging
    endpoint and is not assumed to be identical to a remote specification.
    A missing schema is recorded as unavailable, not silently substituted.

    Resource capture is best effort. CDP cannot always retrieve bodies from
    redirects, active streams or evicted responses; inspect statuses instead
    of assuming complete acquisition.

ENVIRONMENT
    Requires an initialized m environment (nonempty m_ROOT and m_LIB_DIR),
    Node.js >= 22 (native WebSocket) and chromium on effective PATH.
    From a clean shell invoke through: m web-analyze <url> <analysis-path>.
    Browser sandbox and TLS verification remain enabled; no Playwright needed.

SECURITY
    Private run directories and report/evidence files are created with
    restrictive permissions. Raw URLs, HTML, JS, response bodies, browser
    profile, HTTP headers, cookies, browser storage and screenshots may contain
    credentials, personal information
    or signed URLs. Do not commit or share output without review.
    CDP is bound to 127.0.0.1 with an ephemeral port and retains local
    debugger-level access during execution.

EXIT STATUS
    0   Completed and at least one rendered DOM state was observed.
    1   m environment is missing.
    2   Invalid/missing operands.
    3   Invalid URL.
    4   Unsupported URL scheme.
    5   Could not allocate a unique run directory.
    6   m library root unavailable.
    7   Existing web-analyze CDP recorder unavailable.
    8   Chromium capture initialization failed.
    9   Recorder finalization failed.
    10  Markdown report generation failed.
    11  No rendered DOM state observed (inspect partial evidence).
    12  Unexpected execution failure.

EXAMPLE
    m web-analyze https://example.org ./analysis

